WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:08.480
In 1962, your Congress decided that before sick people could take medicine, the medicine needed to spend nearly a decade

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.860
proving it works. The sick people, meanwhile, needed to spend that decade dying.

3
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:24.020
This was called "safety.". The drugs are tested on 3,000 young, healthy volunteers (excluding the sick, elderly, and anyone

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:30.880
on other medications), then prescribed to millions of actual sick people, where the problems finally show up.

5
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.480
It's like testing sunscreen on people who live in caves. A Note on Blame.

6
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:45.580
When this chapter says "the F.D.A. does X," read it as "the 1962 law requires the F.D.A.

7
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.980
to do X.". Congress wrote the rules. The F.D.A. follows them.

8
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:57.420
Blaming the F.D.A. is like blaming the gun instead of the person who loaded it, aimed it at patients, and pulled the trigger

9
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:04.860
on a sixty-two-year schedule. The forty-four point one times Inefficiency Tax.

10
00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:13.140
Compare two real-world systems for testing drugs: The Oxford RECOVERY Trial: Tested COVID treatments on 47,000 patients

11
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.360
for five hundred dollars. Found a life-saving treatment in three months.

12
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:25.980
Like a normal species would. The Post-1962 System: Averages forty-one thousand dollars per trial.

13
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:31.880
Takes nine point one years to approve a new drug. Like humans would.

14
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:38.800
The current system costs forty-four point one times more and takes forty-two longer.

15
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:47.220
That's not inefficiency. That's a system performing exactly as designed, if the design goal was to kill people slowly enough

16
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.420
that nobody notices. The eight point two years Efficacy Lag.

17
00:01:52.980 --> 00:02:01.140
Here's where your lifespan goes to die: Phase One (Safety Testing): two point three years - Tests if the drug kills people.

18
00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:10.520
This part works. Phase Two and Three (Efficacy Testing): eight point two years - Tests if the drug works better than placebo.

19
00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:18.780
This is where people die waiting. The math is simple: If you die in year five and the drug gets approved in year ten, the

20
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:27.320
regulatory process murdered you. That's eight point two years sitting in regulatory purgatory after we already know the

21
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:32.020
drug won't kill you. You invented science yesterday, cosmically speaking.

22
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:39.920
Nearly every study ends with "more research is needed," which is scientist for "please keep paying us while we figure out

23
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.580
what we're looking at.". This would be fine except people are decomposing while you figure it out.

24
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:57.900
What the 1962 Efficacy Requirements Changed. Before 1962, 144 thousand physicians tested treatments on real patients and

25
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:05.620
published results. Life expectancy grew three point eight two years every decade for eighty straight years.

26
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:15.340
Things were going well. Naturally, Congress intervened. Phase One safety testing (added in 1938) worked perfectly.

27
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.920
It prevented all U.S. thalidomide deaths while Europe had thousands.

28
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:27.460
So in 1962, Congress looked at a system that was working and thought, "What if we made this much worse?"

29
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:35.760
They added massive pre-approval efficacy requirements via the Kefauver Harris Amendment, blocking patient access for years

30
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:44.340
during testing. The cost per drug was twenty-four point seven million dollars pre-nineteen sixty-two and two point six zero

31
00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:50.220
billion dollars post-nineteen sixty-two, a one hundred and five times increase.

32
00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:59.000
New approvals were forty-six per year in nineteen sixty and thirteen per year in nineteen sixty-three, a seventy percent

33
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:08.840
decline. Life expectancy growth was three point eight two years per decade pre-nineteen sixty-two and one point five four

34
00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:13.920
years per decade post-nineteen sixty-two, a sixty percent reduction.

35
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:23.240
Time to approval was two to three years pre-nineteen sixty-two and ten point five years post-nineteen sixty-two, which is

36
00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:31.920
five to eight times slower. The temporal break is exact. Every metric got worse the moment the law passed.

37
00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:41.600
Like flipping a switch from "progress" to "paperwork." The one hundred and five times cost increase is rigorously documented.

38
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.320
See Drug Development Cost Analysis for methodology, sensitivity analysis, and source data.

39
00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:56.880
The Modern Consequences. Even if you think historical analysis is just opinions with footnotes, the current system's failures

40
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.100
are undeniable: Scientists discover a cure for your disease.

41
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.640
You wait seventeen years. You die. The F.D.A. approves the cure.

42
00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.700
Pharmaceutical companies charge your widow ten thousand dollars per pill.

43
00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.520
Beautiful system if you're a mortician or bankruptcy lawyer.

44
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.730
Fourteen years from discovery to patient. That's longer than it took to build the pyramids.

45
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:28.340
Those involved moving rocks the size of houses without machinery (also the pharaohs didn't have to file quarterly progress

46
00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:34.180
reports in triplicate). High Costs Kill Innovation, Reward Monopoly.

47
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:43.340
Before nineteen sixty-two: Genius scientist invents cure, raises a few million, tests safety, gives it to people.

48
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:53.080
Like a civilization that wants to survive would. Now: Genius scientist must convince one of three mega-corporations to spend

49
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.240
two point six zero billion dollars on a ten percent chance of success.

50
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.980
Those corporations already sell inferior drugs for the same condition.

51
00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:12.580
The math for the mega-corporation: Option One: Spend two point six zero billion dollars on trials Ninety percent chance:

52
00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:18.460
Lose everything. Ten percent chance: Succeed, then cannibalize your own profitable drug.

53
00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:26.420
Option Two: Buy the patent, put it on a shelf Zero percent chance of losing two point six zero billion dollars.

54
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.900
One hundred percent chance your inferior cash cow keeps printing money.

55
00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.760
Which would you choose if you were a rational sociopath in a suit?

56
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.740
The profit incentive doesn't just fail to reward better treatments.

57
00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:46.380
It actively punishes them. Off-Patent Drugs and Rare Diseases: Mathematically Doomed.

58
00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:55.060
The nineteen sixty-two law made it mathematically impossible to cure diseases that aren't profitable enough: Ninety-five

59
00:06:55.060 --> 00:07:03.100
percent of diseases are rare: Development cost (two point six zero billion dollars) divided by patient population (approximately

60
00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:08.040
ten thousand patients) equals two hundred and sixty thousand dollars per patient.

61
00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:14.280
No patent equals no funding: Off-patent drugs can't attract billion-dollar investments.

62
00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:20.720
Pre-specification kills serendipity: Must predict what drug cures before testing.

63
00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:28.700
When something costs more, you get less of it. This is the first thing they teach in economics, and the last thing anyone

64
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:34.500
considers when writing health policy. Ninety-five percent of diseases have zero treatments.

65
00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.320
Not because cures are impossible. Because cures are unprofitable.

66
00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:48.320
The Actual Death Toll of "Drug Lag". Economists call it "drug lag," which is a lovely, clinical way of saying "people rotting

67
00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:55.300
to death in a queue.". Early estimates: twenty-one thousand to one hundred twenty thousand American lives per decade.

68
00:07:56.060 --> 00:08:01.940
But that was just the U.S. A comprehensive quantitative analysis using W.H.O.

69
00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:11.400
mortality data estimates the total cost of the eight point two years post-safety efficacy delay: four hundred sixteen million

70
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:20.580
deaths eventually avoidable deaths. Including years lived with disability: seven point nine four billion disability-adjusted

71
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:31.540
life years, or D.A.L.Y.s, of healthy human life deleted. Economic cost: one point one nine quadrillion dollars (twenty twenty-four

72
00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:36.419
U.S.D.), thirty-eight times more than all global pharma R. and D.

73
00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:44.820
spending combined. Even if these estimates are off by a factor of ten, the system still costs eighty-two times more per

74
00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.180
patient and takes thirty-six times longer than proven alternatives.

75
00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:56.480
Here's a news story from the Non-Existent Times by No One Ever without a picture of all the people who die from lack of

76
00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.800
access to life-saving treatments that might have been. Types of Error in F.D.A.

77
00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:07.070
Approval Decision If the F.D.A. allows a drug that is beneficial, it is a Correct Decision.

78
00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.020
If the F.D.A. allows a drug that is harmful, victims are identifiable and might appear on Oprah.

79
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:19.840
If the F.D.A. does not allow a drug that is beneficial, victims are not identifiable or acknowledged.

80
00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.500
If the F.D.A. does not allow a drug that is harmful, it is a Correct Decision.

81
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:30.620
The most infamous case: beta-blockers. Europe used them to prevent heart attacks.

82
00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:36.080
The U.S. delayed for a decade, killing an estimated one hundred thousand Americans.

83
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:42.370
More than died in Vietnam and Korea combined. From one drug delay.

84
00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:48.540
After nineteen sixty-two, U.S. life expectancy diverged from Switzerland's (which didn't introduce the same delays).

85
00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:54.840
More drug approvals in the eighties narrowed the gap. Fewer approvals in the nineties widened it again.

86
00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:02.260
How the Incentives Work. F.D.A. Regulator Decision Tree. Approve drug that later shows problems.

87
00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:08.060
Congressional hearing (televised). “F.D.A. APPROVED KILLER DRUG” headlines.

88
00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.040
Career ends. Pension threatened. Delay drug that could save lives.

89
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:18.300
Nothing happens. Dead people don't complain. Promotion on schedule.

90
00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:26.100
Retire to pharma job. This isn't conspiracy. It's just a system where the only career-ending move is doing something.

91
00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:32.180
Approve a bad drug, your name is in the newspaper. Delay a good drug, the people who could have complained are dead.

92
00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:36.230
It's a perfect incentive structure if you're optimizing for cowardice.

93
00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:41.780
The Math: Why Current Regulations Increase Total Harm. where: where: where: where: where: where: where: where: where: where:

94
00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:49.460
where: Even generously crediting ALL drug withdrawals to the 1962 changes (ignoring that Phase I safety testing already

95
00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:58.500
existed), the prevented harm totals approximately 2.59 million disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s.

96
00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:10.520
The delay harm totals 7.94 billion D.A.L.Y.s. For every 1 unit of harm prevented, the current system creates three thousand

97
00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:17.960
and seventy units of harm through delay. Why Bureaucrats Are Rewarded for Letting You Die.

98
00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:25.420
Dr. Henry I. Miller ran the F.D.A. team reviewing recombinant human insulin in the early 1980s.

99
00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:31.380
Mountains of evidence showed it was safe and effective. His supervisor refused to approve it.

100
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.780
If someone died from the drug, heads would roll. If people died waiting for the drug?

101
00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:42.760
Nothing happens. Dead patients don't testify before Congress.

102
00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:50.480
Clinical Trial Theater: Excluding 86.1 percent of Reality Makes Drugs More Dangerous.

103
00:11:52.140 --> 00:12:00.560
The F.D.A. requires rigorous trials to ensure safety. Then it tests the drugs on people who aren't sick, aren't old, aren't

104
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:08.320
on other drugs, and aren't pregnant. Basically, it tests drugs on triathletes and then acts surprised when grandma has a

105
00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:17.540
reaction. Trials under the current system exclude: Patients over 65: Most people who actually take medications (excluded

106
00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:24.180
due to "comorbidities"). Patients under 18: All children (metabolism differs from adults).

107
00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.620
Pregnant women: Excluded entirely (then drugs prescribed during pregnancy anyway).

108
00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:35.900
Anyone with comorbidities: The sickest patients most likely to have adverse reactions.

109
00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:42.260
Anyone on other medications: Everyone elderly (can't detect drug interactions).

110
00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:46.720
Anyone too far from trial sites: Poor and rural populations.

111
00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:54.920
Result: In antidepressant trials alone, 86.1 percent of real patients are excluded.

112
00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:03.320
The drugs are tested on the healthiest people alive, declared "safe," then handed to everyone the trial specifically avoided.

113
00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:13.380
This is how Vioxx, Fen-Phen, and Bextra all got approved, celebrated, prescribed to millions, and then withdrawn after killing

114
00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:17.440
people whose demographics the trials had carefully excluded.

115
00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:25.140
Testing drugs only on healthy volunteers to ensure safety is like testing parachutes only on the ground.

116
00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:32.000
No Long-Term Outcome Data. Data collection can be as short as several months.

117
00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:39.180
You take the drug for forty years. Pre-Specification Requirements Kill Innovation.

118
00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:47.240
The regulations require drug developers to predict exactly what a treatment will cure before testing it on humans.

119
00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:51.100
You must know the answer before you're allowed to ask the question.

120
00:13:52.120 --> 00:14:00.820
This is how science works on your planet, apparently. In 2007, Dendreon showed that its immunotherapy drug Provenge significantly

121
00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:06.520
reduced deaths from prostate cancer. The F.D.A. advisory committee agreed it worked.

122
00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:12.000
Then the F.D.A. rejected it anyway. Not because it didn't save lives.

123
00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:17.340
Because Dendreon filled out the wrong form about which lives it was planning to save.

124
00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:24.680
"People stopped dying" wasn't good enough. The paperwork predicting that people would stop dying wasn't filed in the right

125
00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:33.100
order. Three more years and another trial were required. During those three years, people who could have stopped dying continued

126
00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:41.380
dying, but at least the forms were correct. Due to these additional costs, Dendreon ultimately filed for chapter eleven

127
00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:50.320
bankruptcy. The Negative Results Black Hole. Here's something that would be illegal if anybody important cared: Negative

128
00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:58.040
trial results published: thirty-seven percent. Positive trial results published: ninety-four percent.

129
00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:04.160
Money wasted repeating failed experiments: approximately one hundred billion dollars annually.

130
00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:11.580
Companies are allowed to hide when drugs don't work, which is like letting restaurants hide their health inspection failures.

131
00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:18.560
Actually, restaurants can't do that. Drugs get less transparency than a sandwich shop.

132
00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.680
Company: "We tested this drug!". Regulator: "Did it work?".

133
00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:32.680
Company: "...We tested this drug!". Pharmaceutical companies bury negative results deeper than Jimmy Hoffa.

134
00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:41.860
Other companies waste billions testing the same dead ends. Your insurance premiums fund this magnificent inefficiency.

135
00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:49.020
It's like casinos only having to report when people win. Countries That Don't Have Our "Safety".

136
00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.500
Japan gives conditional approval after Phase Two with real-world data collection.

137
00:15:55.180 --> 00:16:00.140
Time to patient: two to three years. Americans fly there for treatment.

138
00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:06.420
The E.U. lets terminal patients access experimental drugs at a doctor's discretion.

139
00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:16.200
F.D.A.: "But what if they die?" (They're already dying.). Right to Try (U.S.) passed despite F.D.A.

140
00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:20.520
opposition. The F.D.A. made it effectively impossible to use.

141
00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:27.640
Patients helped: less than two hundred total. Patients who wanted help: tens of thousands.

142
00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:38.120
The COVID Test Fiasco. January 2020: the World Health Organization, or W.H.O., develops COVID test, world starts using it.

143
00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:44.680
February: F.D.A. blocks all non-C.D.C. tests to "ensure quality.".

144
00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:50.160
The C.D.C. tests were contaminated. Private labs begged to help.

145
00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:55.800
Late March: F.D.A. finally allows other tests after thousands die.

146
00:16:56.620 --> 00:17:03.760
The only approved tests didn't work. Ensuring quality control of broken tests is like T.S.A.

147
00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:07.680
confiscating water bottles while letting actual weapons through.

148
00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:16.520
Except people died. Small Trials Are Dangerous. Phase Three trials test one thousand to three thousand patients.

149
00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:23.780
A one-in-ten-thousand adverse event is mathematically invisible in a trial of three thousand.

150
00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:32.580
Then the drug goes to millions. After approval, the F.D.A. monitors safety using the Food and Drug Administration Adverse

151
00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:42.140
Event Reporting System, or F.A.E.R.S., a system where doctors voluntarily report problems if they feel like it.

152
00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.840
It captures less than ten percent of actual adverse events.

153
00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:54.240
Vioxx killed an estimated thirty-eight thousand to fifty-five thousand Americans before this honor system noticed.

154
00:17:55.160 --> 00:18:02.840
That's more people than died in the entire Korean War, detected by a system that relies on doctors filling out optional

155
00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:13.760
paperwork. A D.F.D.A. (the decentralized F.D.A.) with ten million participants catches one-in-ten-thousand reactions, includes

156
00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:21.240
real populations, tracks long-term outcomes, and provides automated surveillance instead of voluntary paperwork.

157
00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:29.180
The current system tests 3,000 cherry-picked patients, then hopes doctors voluntarily fill out forms when their patients

158
00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:35.300
drop dead. The alternative is monitoring millions of real patients automatically.

159
00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:41.400
One of these is a safety system. The other is a prayer. The Bottom Line.

160
00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:51.440
Post-safety efficacy lag is eight point two years. Deaths from the lag are four hundred sixteen million deaths.

161
00:18:52.760 --> 00:19:00.860
disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s destroyed are seven point nine four billion D.A.L.Y.s.

162
00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:08.480
Economic cost is one point one nine quadrillion dollars. Harm ratio (delay vs.

163
00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:14.620
prevention) is three thousand and seventy. Phase One safety testing works.

164
00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:19.620
Keep it. The rest is a 62-year-old mistake dressed up in a lab coat.

165
00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:28.160
Replace the eight point two-year post-safety efficacy delay with real-world evidence collection.

166
00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:36.940
The only thing missing is the political will to admit that a law signed before the Beatles existed might not be the pinnacle

167
00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:45.720
of pharmaceutical regulation. Technical Analysis. For the full quantitative analysis including methodology, sensitivity

168
00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:55.160
analysis, and source data (warning: contains numbers large enough to make you physically ill), see: The Human Cost of Regulatory

